load As0, Bs0;
syncthread;
compute;

for (int t = 1; t < numTiles; ++t) {
    load As1, Bs1 (overlaps with computation);
    // computation uses As0, Bs0
    syncthread;
    compute (now uses As1, Bs1);
    syncthread;
    swap;
}
compute (last tile);
```

Wait, this doesn't reduce syncthreads. The syncthreads are needed for synchronization.

Actually, let me think about this differently. The syncthread is only needed when:
1. All threads have finished loading before computation starts
2. Computation finishes before loading the next tile

In the current code, both syncthreads are necessary:
```
load;  // must wait for all threads to finish loading
syncthread;
compute;  // can overlap with next load? NO - shared memory is reused
syncthread;  // must wait for computation to finish before next load
load;  // reuse shared memory
```

With double buffering, we can overlap loading and computation:
```
