Wait, this is the same structure. The key insight for double buffering is:

```
// Tile 0: load and compute
load tile 0;
syncthread;
compute tile 0;

for tile t = 1..n:
    load tile t;  // overlaps with compute of t-1 on GPU
    syncthread;
    compute tile t;
    syncthread;
```

The "overlap" is that the load of tile t starts while the compute of tile t-1 is still running. But `__syncthreads()` blocks all threads, so the load of tile t can only start after all threads have finished computing tile t-1. There's no real overlap.

Unless... the GPU can schedule the load of tile t before the compute of tile t-1 completes, because they're different shared memory sets. But with the same shared memory arrays (no double buffering), the load of tile t overwrites shared memory while tile t-1's compute is reading it. This is a data race.

So double buffering requires TWO sets of shared memory arrays. Then:
- Load tile t+1 into As1, Bs1 (different set from As0, Bs0)
- Compute tile t from As0, Bs0

These can overlap because they use different shared memory sets.

But the `__syncthreads()` after the compute blocks all threads. So the load of tile t+1 can't start until all threads have finished computing tile t. There's still no overlap.

Wait, actually, the `__syncthreads()` is AFTER the compute. So:

```
compute tile t from As0, Bs0;
__syncthreads();  // all threads finished computing
load tile t+1 into As1, Bs1;  // starts after syncthread
```

The load of tile t+1 can only start after the syncthread. So there's no overlap with the compute of tile t.

Unless the load starts EARLY, before the syncthread:

```
load tile t+1 into As1, Bs1;  // starts while other threads are computing
compute tile t from As0, Bs0;
__syncthreads();  // wait for both load and compute to finish
```

But this has a race condition! Thread A is computing tile t from As0 while thread B is loading tile t+1 into As1. If As0 and As1 are the same array, there's a race. If they're different arrays (double buffering), there's no race on the data, but the GPU might not execute them concurrently.

